<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12653v1/dataset_st.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Statistics of the SAMM. (a) The distribution of manipulation types and the distribution of source data; (b) The distribution of gender and occupations among celebrities involved in swap manipulation; (c) The word cloud of emotional descriptions for attribute manipulation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attribute Manipulation.</span> As shown in figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Related work ‣ Beyond Artificial Misalignment: Detecting and Grounding Semantic-Coordinated Multimodal Manipulations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we employ the HFGI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and StyleCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patashnik et al., 2021</a>)</cite> for visual emotion editing and large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for textual emotion manipulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Face attribute edit.</span></em> We first utilize the DSFD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2019</a>)</cite> model for face detection to randomly select a target face <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, recording its bounding box coordinates <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1},x_{2},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1},x_{2},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The Qwen2-VL model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> predicts the emotional state of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then randomly fed into HFGI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> or StyleCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patashnik et al., 2021</a>)</cite> to generate an opposite emotional manipulation (e.g., modifying "happy" to "sad"), resulting in <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.We then replace <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Text distortion.</span></em> Following the same operation as in the swap manipulation, we utilize Qwen2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and Qwen2-vl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to complete the matching of the name <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the face <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. To achieve diverse emotional expressions, we collect multiple emotions and provide various expressions for each emotion option <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=\{e_{i}|e_{i}=[w^{i}_{1},w^{i}_{2},...]\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{E}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=\{e_{i}|e_{i}=[w^{i}_{1},w^{i}_{2},...]\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{E}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the 1-th expression word for emotion <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently,
we utilize Qwen2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to incorporate the randomly selected expression of opposite emotion predicted by the Qwen2-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> into <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> without altering the event described in the news, thereby obtaining <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, accompanied by a one-hot vector <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathit{label}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑙𝑎𝑏𝑒𝑙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathit{label}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to indicate whether the <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th word is tampered. To ensure accuracy, we conducted manual sampling inspections on the tasks completed by Qwen2.5 and Qwen2-VL. (Implementation details of Qwen2.5 and Qwen2-VL for these tasks, along with accuracy validation, are provided in the appendix.)</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Dataset Statistics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The SAMM dataset (260,970 samples) captures real-world tampering patterns with comprehensive annotations. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Multi-Modal Manipulation ‣ 3. SAMM Dataset ‣ Beyond Artificial Misalignment: Detecting and Grounding Semantic-Coordinated Multimodal Manipulations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a) and Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2. Multi-Modal Manipulation ‣ 3. SAMM Dataset ‣ Beyond Artificial Misalignment: Detecting and Grounding Semantic-Coordinated Multimodal Manipulations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it includes: 111K original news, 80K swap-manipulated, and 69K attribute-manipulated cases. Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Multi-Modal Manipulation ‣ 3. SAMM Dataset ‣ Beyond Artificial Misalignment: Detecting and Grounding Semantic-Coordinated Multimodal Manipulations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) highlights celebrity diversity in swap manipulation, while Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2. Multi-Modal Manipulation ‣ 3. SAMM Dataset ‣ Beyond Artificial Misalignment: Detecting and Grounding Semantic-Coordinated Multimodal Manipulations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c)’s emotion word clouds demonstrate expressional diversity in attribute manipulation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1. CAP-aided Context-aware Encoding ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Beyond Artificial Misalignment: Detecting and Grounding Semantic-Coordinated Multimodal Manipulations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> depicts the framework of RamDG.
Specifically, given multimodal news, the headshot and metadata of the person mentioned in the text are first retrieved from CAP. Next, the multimodal input and retrieved auxiliary input(s) first pass through the CAP-aided Context-aware Encoding, which generates uni-modal embeddings. Subsequently, these embeddings are fed into the Cross-modal Feature Fusion to achieve information fusion across multiple modalities. Image Forgery Grounding, Text Manipulation Localization, Fake News Recognition, and Manipulation Type Recognition are then performed respectively to achieve manipulation detection and grounding. Finally, the overall network is optimized by a combination of grounding and detection losses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Celebrity Attribute Retrieval.</span> Given the image-text pair <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(I,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(I,T)</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ a string matching algorithm to rapidly retrieve associated external knowledge from CAP using the person names in <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, obtaining a set of related pairs <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{P_{j}=(I_{j},T_{j})|j=1,2,\dots,P_{j}\in\mathit{CAP}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐶𝐴𝑃</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{j}=(I_{j},T_{j})|j=1,2,\dots,P_{j}\in\mathit{CAP}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as the auxiliary knowledge.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>CAP-aided Context-aware Encoding</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image Fusion with External Celebrities.</span> To effectively incorporate the retrieved person images, we first patchify <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then input them into a Transformer-based Vision Encoder <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, obtaining <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{v}(I)=\mathit{V}=\{\mathit{V}^{cls},\mathit{V}^{pat}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{v}(I)=\mathit{V}=\{\mathit{V}^{cls},\mathit{V}^{pat}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{v}(I_{j})=\mathit{V}_{j}=\{\mathit{V}_{j}^{cls},\mathit{V}_{j}^{pat}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{v}(I_{j})=\mathit{V}_{j}=\{\mathit{V}_{j}^{cls},\mathit{V}_{j}^{pat}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{V}^{pat}=\{\mathit{V}^{1},\mathit{V}^{2},\cdots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{V}^{pat}=\{\mathit{V}^{1},\mathit{V}^{2},\cdots\}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{V}_{j}^{pat}=\{\mathit{V}_{j}^{1},\mathit{V}_{j}^{2},\cdots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{V}_{j}^{pat}=\{\mathit{V}_{j}^{1},\mathit{V}_{j}^{2},\cdots\}</annotation></semantics></math> are the corresponding patch embeddings.
To endow the raw multimodal inputs with the knowledge of the focused celebrities, we fuse the features within modalities to enhance the representative. In particular, for image input <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>,
we first concatenates the patches <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathit{V}_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathit{V}_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of all retrieved celebrities to obtain <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{V}_{cb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{V}_{cb}</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, we query features from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{cb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{cb}</annotation></semantics></math> using image feature <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and then perform a cross-attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite> to equip the celebrities knowledge to the raw image feature:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathit{V}_{f}&amp;=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{V},K=\mathit{V}_{cb},V=\mathit{V}_{cb}|\Theta_{1})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathit{V}_{f}&amp;=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{V},K=\mathit{V}_{cb},V=\mathit{V}_{cb}|\Theta_{1})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{V}_{f}=\{\mathit{V}_{f}^{cls},\mathit{V}_{f}^{pat}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{V}_{f}=\{\mathit{V}_{f}^{cls},\mathit{V}_{f}^{pat}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the resultant comprehensive features. Attn(<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>) is the multi-head attention function and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the parameters in the Attn(<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text Encoding with Celebrity Notes.</span> For text input, as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> have high information density and no redundancy, we directly append the celebrity meta text(s) to the text, thereby directly enriching the textual context. Subsequently, the enhanced text is fed into text encoder <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus the compresive representative for text is obtained, marked as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{L}_{f}=E_{t}(T_{all})=\{\mathit{L}_{f}^{cls},\mathit{L}_{f}^{tok}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{L}_{f}=E_{t}(T_{all})=\{\mathit{L}_{f}^{cls},\mathit{L}_{f}^{tok}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pure text feature <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\{L^{cls},L^{tok}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\{L^{cls},L^{tok}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and celebrity textual feature <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=\{L_{f}^{cls},L_{f}^{tok}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=\{L_{f}^{cls},L_{f}^{tok}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are also obtained by feeding the text and the concatenated celebrity notes to text encoder.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12653v1/ramdg.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/232;" width="476" height="232" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>The architecture of our proposed RamDG. It processes image-text pairs and CAP-retrieved knowledge using uni-Encoders and the CNCL module, then fuses knowledge-enriched embeddings for visual/textual manipulation localization, fake news recognition, and manipulation type prediction.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Celebrity-News Contrastive Learning.</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human usually doubts a piece of news when they found conflicts between the news and the information they know.
Motivated by this consideration, Celebrity-News Contrastive Learning (CNCL) conducts a contrastive learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2020</a>)</cite> procedure between the multimodal news and auxiliary celebrity information, aiming to endow the network a human-like reasoning ability for fake news detection.
In specific, we adopt a contrastive learning mechanism to simulate human logical reasoning : by aligning the semantics of untampered celebrity information with the news, we enhance the model’s detection ability:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{v2v}(I_{j},I,\mathcal{I})=-\log\bigg(\frac{\exp(s(I_{j},I^{+})/\tau)}{\sum_{I_{k}\in\mathcal{I}}\exp(s(I_{j},I_{k})/\tau)}\bigg)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{v2v}(I_{j},I,\mathcal{I})=-\log\bigg(\frac{\exp(s(I_{j},I^{+})/\tau)}{\sum_{I_{k}\in\mathcal{I}}\exp(s(I_{j},I_{k})/\tau)}\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the cosine similarity function, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the learnable parameter controlling the temperature, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of the j-th celebrity appearing in the news <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,T)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding positive sample news image, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of a positive sample news image <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and multiple negative samples that do not include the celebrity represented by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We map the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[CLS]</span> token through a projection layer and incorporate it into the cosine similarity calculation as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(I_{j},I)=[P_{v}(\mathit{V}_{j}^{\mathit{cls}})]^{T}\hat{P}_{v}(\hat{V}^{\mathit{cls}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(I_{j},I)=[P_{v}(\mathit{V}_{j}^{\mathit{cls}})]^{T}\hat{P}_{v}(\hat{V}^{\mathit{cls}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathit{V}}^{cls}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathit{V}}^{cls}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[CLS]</span> token obtained by encoding <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> with momentum encoder <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{E}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{E}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are the mapping layers. Similarly, the contrastive learning <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{v2t}(I_{j},T,\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{v2t}(I_{j},T,\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> between external knowledge images and news text can be performed.
In a analogous fashion, we further augment the contrastive learning on the text side, introducing <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t2v}(T_{j},I,\mathcal{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t2v}(T_{j},I,\mathcal{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t2t}(T_{j},T,\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t2t}(T_{j},T,\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. (The specific expressions for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{v2t},\mathcal{L}_{t2v},\mathcal{L}_{t2t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{v2t},\mathcal{L}_{t2v},\mathcal{L}_{t2t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be found in the appendix).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_indent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the overall loss function of Celebrity-News Contrastive Learning mechanism is:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{cncl}=\mathcal{L}_{v2v}+\mathcal{L}_{v2t}+\mathcal{L}_{t2v}+\mathcal{L}_{t2t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{cncl}=\mathcal{L}_{v2v}+\mathcal{L}_{v2t}+\mathcal{L}_{t2v}+\mathcal{L}_{t2t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Image Forgery Grounding via FVRM</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To integrate the knowledge of both modalities, we first fuse the information from two modalities to obtain a hybrid multimodal representation with comprehensive contexts, where the text <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the query to collect clues from image via attention:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathit{f}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{V}_{f},K=\mathit{L}_{f},V=\mathit{L}_{f}|\Theta_{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathit{f}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{V}_{f},K=\mathit{L}_{f},V=\mathit{L}_{f}|\Theta_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathit{f}}=\{\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}},\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{tok}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathit{f}}=\{\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}},\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{tok}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since visual manipulation is small-scale and localized, we need to extract local semantic information related to visual tampering. To achieve this, we adopt the Fine-grained Visual Refinement Mechanism (FVRM).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-grained Visual Refinement Mechanism</span>. Patches in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{pat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{pat}</annotation></semantics></math> from tampered regions differ semantically from those in unaltered regions, revealing local tampering traces. Based on this observation, we add a classification head (three linear layers) after <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\text{pat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mtext>pat</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\text{pat}}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict patch manipulation. The loss <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{pat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>pat</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{pat}}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed using cross-entropy. Specifically, the loss function is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{pat}=-\sum_{i=1}^{C}\left[y_{pat}\log(P_{pat})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{pat}=-\sum_{i=1}^{C}\left[y_{pat}\log(P_{pat})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{pat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{pat}</annotation></semantics></math> is the label converted from bbox coordinates to indicate whether a patch is manipulated. <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{pat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{pat}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability, predicted by the model, that the patch has been tampered with. <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of patches in an image. Under the supervision of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{pat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{pat}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{V}^{pat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{V}^{pat}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped through the first two linear layers of classification head to obtain <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{V}^{pat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{V}^{pat}</annotation></semantics></math>, which contains visual manipulation traces. <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{cls}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{cls}</annotation></semantics></math> contains semantic information related to the global examination of the detected news pair. By fusing <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{V}^{pat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{V}^{pat}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{cls}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{cls}</annotation></semantics></math>, the resulting <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> captures both global and local information. The process is as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\text{cls}},\,K=\widetilde{V}^{\text{pat}},\,V=\widetilde{V}^{\text{pat}}|\Theta_{3})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mtext>cls</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>pat</mtext></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>pat</mtext></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\text{cls}},\,K=\widetilde{V}^{\text{pat}},\,V=\widetilde{V}^{\text{pat}}|\Theta_{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> perform attention computation with a learnable vector <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to capture semantic information at different scales. The fusion of external knowledge in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathit{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathit{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> dilutes the local details of the original image. To address this, we perform residual connection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{M}_{\mathit{fv}}=M_{\mathit{f}}+\mathit{V}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>𝑓𝑣</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{M}_{\mathit{fv}}=M_{\mathit{f}}+\mathit{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, cross-attention is performed between <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{M}_{\mathit{fv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>𝑓𝑣</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{M}_{\mathit{fv}}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve fine-grained visual refinement, denoted as <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{fv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝑓𝑣</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{fv}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The specific process is as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathcal{Q},\,K=\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}},V=\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}|\Theta_{4})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathcal{Q},\,K=\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}},V=\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}}|\Theta_{4})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{fv}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}},K={M}_{\mathit{fv}},V={M}_{\mathit{fv}}|\Theta_{5})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝑓𝑣</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>𝑓𝑣</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>𝑓𝑣</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{fv}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{f}},K={M}_{\mathit{fv}},V={M}_{\mathit{fv}}|\Theta_{5})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We use the obtained <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{fv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝑓𝑣</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\mathit{fv}}</annotation></semantics></math> for bounding box prediction. The <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> loss and IoU loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rezatofighi et al., 2019</a>)</cite> between the predicted boxes and the bounding box coordinates are then computed:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{bbox}}=-||P_{gro}-\text{Sigmoid}(y_{box})||_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝑏𝑏𝑜𝑥</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>Sigmoid</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{bbox}}=-||P_{gro}-\text{Sigmoid}(y_{box})||_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{IoU}}(P_{gro},\text{Sigmoid}(\mathit{y}_{box}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝐼𝑜𝑈</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>Sigmoid</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{IoU}}(P_{gro},\text{Sigmoid}(\mathit{y}_{box}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{IoU}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝐼𝑜𝑈</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{IoU}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the IoU loss function, <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{gro}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{gro}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the predicted bounding box coordinates from the model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Deep Manipulation Detection</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text Manipulation Localization.</span> This section achieves fine-grained text manipulation localization: predicting whether each word in <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced or added.
We first fuse the textual representation <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{L}^{\mathit{tok}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{L}^{\mathit{tok}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the visual semantic feature <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{V}_{\mathit{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{V}_{\mathit{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> through cross-attention to generate a fine-grained cross-modal feature <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{L}^{\mathit{tok}},K=\mathit{V}_{\mathit{f}},V=\mathit{V}_{\mathit{f}}|\Theta_{5})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{L}^{\mathit{tok}},K=\mathit{V}_{\mathit{f}},V=\mathit{V}_{\mathit{f}}|\Theta_{5})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}}</annotation></semantics></math> can locate tokens in <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}</annotation></semantics></math> with abnormal logical relationships due to its global semantic information, which are often added or modified. Therefore, we further allow <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}}</annotation></semantics></math> to extract information from <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The above process is as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}},K=\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}},V=\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}}|\Theta_{6})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}=\text{Attn}(Q=\mathit{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}},K=\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}},V=\mathit{M}_{\mathit{f}}^{\mathit{cls}}|\Theta_{6})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we use <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{L}_{\mathit{v}}^{\mathit{tok}}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the probability of manipulation for each token, and construct the text manipulation localization loss function <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{tok}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{tok}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the cross-entropy loss function:</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{tok}}=-\sum_{i}\left[y_{tok}^{i}\log(P_{tok}^{i})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{tok}}=-\sum_{i}\left[y_{tok}^{i}\log(P_{tok}^{i})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\mathit{tok}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝑡𝑜𝑘</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\mathit{tok}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the label indicating whether i-th token is manipulated. <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{tok}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{tok}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability, as output by the model, that the i-th token has been tampered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fake News Recognition.</span> This section is used to determine whether <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,T)</annotation></semantics></math> has been tampered. We utilize <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{f}^{\mathit{cls}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{f}^{\mathit{cls}}</annotation></semantics></math> for recognition, as it integrates multi-modal news data and external knowledge from CAP to provide a comprehensive summary. We compute the loss using a cross-entropy function, as detailed below:</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{2cls}=-y_{2cls}\log(P_{2cls})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{2cls}=-y_{2cls}\log(P_{2cls})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2cls}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2cls}</annotation></semantics></math> is the binary classification label and <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2cls}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2cls}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability, predicted by the model, that <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,T)</annotation></semantics></math> has been tampered with.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maniputation Type Recognition.</span> SAMM incorporates three types of tampering traces, including <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V</span>isual <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>wap(VS) manipulation, <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V</span>isual <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>ttribute(VA) manipulation, <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>extual <span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>ttribute(TA) manipulation (including name swapping, and the addition or alteration of emotions). We predict the specific tampering method used in the given news. Consistent with Fake News Recognition, we uses <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{f}^{\mathit{cls}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{f}^{\mathit{cls}}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict and compute the loss using a cross-entropy function, as detailed below:</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{mcls}}=-\sum_{i=1}^{4}\left[y_{mcls}^{i}\log(P_{mcls}^{i})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝑚𝑐𝑙𝑠</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathit{mcls}}=-\sum_{i=1}^{4}\left[y_{mcls}^{i}\log(P_{mcls}^{i})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{y}_{mcls}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{y}_{mcls}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the label of the i-th tampering method used in the given news. <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{mcls}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{mcls}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability, predicted by the model, that the i-th tampering method has been used.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Comparison of multi-modal learning methods for SAMM. We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of models’ performance across four key tasks: binary classification, multi-label classification, image grounding, and text grounding.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Binary Cls(BC)</th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Multi-Label Cls(MLC)</th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Image Grounding(IG)</th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Text Grounding(TG)</th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methods</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">AUC</th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">EER</th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">ACC</th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">mAP</th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">CF1</th>
